Notes

1.The outcome in Wood strikes most people as inequitable. Why? Is it because of the existence of the joint and several liability rule? Is it because the plaintiff is much more at fault than Disney? Is it because the jury has actually assigned percentages to each of the parties, highlighting the exact relative degree of fault between the defendants? Is it about Disney’s contribution rights against its co-defendant? Is it some combination of the above? If this outcome is inequitable, what alternatives are available to avoid this result? The following notes and the materials on contribution that follow will help you answer these questions.

2.Developments following the adoption of comparative fault. Unlike Florida, many states moved away from full joint and several liability when they adopted comparative fault. Instead, in many situations each defendant is only “severally” liable. Under “several liability” the fact finder will be instructed to allocate responsibility for the injuries among the parties and each party will only be responsible for that part of the injuries for which it has been assigned responsibility. As indicated in Wood, the difference becomes critical when one or more parties cannot pay or is legally immune from paying its share of the damages assigned. Note, however, that the changes in the law discussed here only apply when the defendants act independently of each other. If true joint action is found as in the case of a joint enterprise, all jurisdictions still apply joint and several liability.

The Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability has categorized the variety ways that states have responded to the rule of joint and several liability, how it operates when plaintiff has also been apportioned fault, and how responsibility is to be allocated. Thus, the question “what is the effect of comparative fault on defendants’ liability” has a variety of answers.

a.Several liability. Several states have resolved that the adoption of comparative fault leads to a complete repudiation of joint and several liability, even when the plaintiff is not contributorily negligent. See, e.g., Dix & Assoc. Pipeline Contractors, Inc. v. Key, 799 S.W.2d 24 (Ky.1990); McIntyre v. Balentine, 833 S.W.2d 52 (Tenn.1992); Kan. Stat. Ann. § 60–258(a) (1994); Utah Code Ann. § 78–5–818 (2008); Vt. Stat. Ann. tit. 12 § 1036 (1973).

Others states, either as a result of case law or legislation, have abolished joint and several liability only when the plaintiff is partially at fault. Recall this was the alternative suggested by Chief Justice McDonald in his Wood dissent. See, e.g., Price v. Southwestern Bell Tel. Co., 812 P.2d 1355 (Okla.‌1991); Ga. Code Ann. § 51–12–33 (2000). The rationale for this distinction rests upon the idea that if the plaintiff is not at fault at all, the plaintiff should still be able to recover all her damages from any of the responsible defendants. However if the plaintiff is also at fault, the plaintiff should bear at least some of the consequences of the inability to collect a share from another responsible party.

b.Threshold tests. Some states abolished joint and several liability for defendants whose share of the responsibility is found to be below a certain
805
percentage. See Tex. Civ. Pract. & Rem. Code § 33.013 (with a few exceptions, joint and several liability applies only to defendants assigned more than 50% of the responsibility); 735 Ill. Compiled Stat. § 5/2–1117 (no joint and several liability for defendants found less than 25% responsible). It is said that threshold tests are the most widely adopted solution.

After Wood, the Florida legislature adopted a multi-tiered system of threshold tests, see Fla. Stat. § 768.81 (2003), then three years later abandoned this system for a several liability system.

c.Joint and several liability for economic damages. Some states have retained joint and several liability for economic damages but abolished it in whole or in part for non-economic damages, such as pain and suffering. For example, in Iowa joint and several liability is abolished for those defendants “who are found to bear less than fifty percent of the total fault assigned to all parties. However, a defendant found to bear fifty percent or more of fault shall only be jointly and severally liable for economic damages and not for any noneconomic damage awards.” Iowa Code Ann. § 668.4 (West). See also N.Y. McKinney’s Civ.Prac.Law §§ 1600, 1601. Cal. Civil Code § 1431.2 (West Supp. 2003).

The end result of this multitude of variations is that it is almost impossible to generalize about the applicable law. An appendix to Section 17 of the Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability sets out the status of the law in each state on several of these matters in a series of tables reflecting the law as of the date of publication in 2000.

3.The consequence of the choice. The critical question, of course, is what difference does it make whether joint and several or several liability is the applicable law in an indivisible injury case? If all of the defendants are parties and are solvent, then the plaintiff will be fully compensated under each approach, less the state’s treatment of the plaintiff’s own responsibility. The primary function of the alternatives is to set forth what happens if one or more of the defendants is not able to cover its share of the damages.

Under traditional joint and several liability, an insolvent defendant’s share of the judgment falls to the remaining defendants and they bear the risk of a co-tortfeasor’s inability to pay, with a right of contribution. The shares of jointly liable defendants were assessed pro rata because there was no apportionment. Under several liability, the risk of a defendant’s inability to pay lies with the plaintiff—defendants are only ever responsible to pay their apportioned share of the judgment. A contribution action is not needed.

When joint and several liability is retained, and a plaintiff has been apportioned fault, what happens when a defendant is unable to pay his apportioned share? In Wood, the court held that Disney was responsible for the full 86 percent of the judgment, even though Disney was only 1% at fault. A few states have adopted reallocation schemes instead. The idea is a simple one. When a judgment cannot be collected against a defendant, the shortfall is reallocated among all the other parties based on their relative percentage of the total liability, including a plaintiff. Thus, for example, if a plaintiff and two
806
defendants each were held to be one-third responsible for an accident and one defendant proved to be judgment proof, the shortfall would be reallocated among the remaining defendant and the plaintiff in proportion to their comparative percentage of responsibility. In this example, the shortfall would be assigned half to the plaintiff and half to the remaining defendant. This is the solution adopted by the Uniform Contribution Act and the Uniform Apportionment of Tort Responsibility Act (2002). Reallocation statutes have a variety of complicating features. See Ore. Rev. Stat. § 31.610 (3) (no reallocation if defendant’s share of fault is less than plaintiff’s).

4.Divisible v. indivisible injury. One issue that comes up in either joint and several or several liability situations is whether there is a single cause of action against multiple defendants because the injury is indivisible or whether plaintiff simply has separate causes of action against each defendant because the injuries are divisible and assignable to each defendant individually. The law is clear that if the injuries are simultaneous and indivisible, a single action arises out of the occurrence, even when the defendants acted independently of each other. Moreover, the approach has generally been adopted even when the injuries were inflicted sequentially, so long as it is impossible as a practical matter to identify which defendant caused which injury. The issue comes up often in sequential automobile accidents. Take for example the relatively common example of defendant A negligently running a red light causing a collision with B who is then hit from behind by defendant C who was following too closely. Assume B has suffered head injuries and a broken leg. It is practically impossible to say which injuries were caused by A and which by C. For examples of the application of this approach, see, e.g., Maddux v. Donaldson, 108 N.W.2d 33 (Mich. 1961); Mathews v. Mills, 178 N.W.2d 841 (Minn. 1970). See generally, Dan Dobbs, The Law of Torts 422–423 (2000). Dobbs also points out that the burden of proof is shifted to the defendant to prove that the damages are separable. Id.

Sometimes injuries are divisible, even in the scenarios discussed above. For example, one may know that in a situation such as that in the Maddux case the plaintiff’s leg was broken in the first crash with defendant A. How should courts proceed when injuries are in fact divisible? The Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability § 26 permits divisibility when there is a reasonable basis for the factfinder to determine both a percentage of responsibility for less than the entire damages and the amount of those separately caused damages.

5.Contribution actions. As noted above, at common law there was no right on the part of one defendant, held liable for an injury, to get contribution or partial payment of the judgment from another defendant, even though the other defendant had also been held liable. However, in most American jurisdictions the rule against contribution has now been abolished or modified. This has occurred principally by statute but in some jurisdictions by judicial decision. In spite of early limitations on who may be a contribution defendant, most jurisdictions now permit contribution actions among joint tortfeasors to
807
the original judgment and against those who may not have been joined in the original judgment.

Most jurisdictions adopted some form of the Uniform Contribution Among Tortfeasors Act, originally promulgated in 1955,5 which permits contribution when a tortfeasor has had to pay more than his share of the common liability. The contribution action in many jurisdictions has been subsumed within the comparative fault/apportionment schemes because the resolution of the joint and several liability issue is a predicate to determining whether contribution is necessary, and how it should be applied.

Where a contribution action continues to be available, a number of questions must be answered. (1) How are the shares determined? (2) What happens when there is an unknown defendant? (3) What is the effect of settlement? (3) What procedures, such as set-off, are permitted?

a.How are shares determined? Prior to the movement to comparative fault, recall that the fact finder only determined whether or not a party was at fault and made no effort to assign a percentage of responsibility. Thus a right of contribution, if it existed at all, could only be done on a pro rata share basis. If there are two liable defendants, each must contribute half of the plaintiff’s damages. If there are three liable defendants, each must contribute one-third of the damages, etc. With the adoption of comparative responsibility, most jurisdictions quickly moved to an allocation of shares based on the fact finder’s determination of the parties’ percentage of responsibility. For example, in Sitzes v. Anchor Motor Freight, Inc., 289 S.E.2d 679 (W. Va. 1982), in response to a certified question from a federal district court, the West Virginia Supreme Court replied that its decision in Bradley v. Appalachian Power Co. dictated this outcome. See Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability § 23.

As discussed in Note 3 above, if a tortfeasor defaults on his obligation to pay for his apportioned share, the comparative fault scheme must determine what happens to the defaulted share. Additional reasons may explain a defendant’s default from responsibility including immunity from suit, such as intra-family immunity (which occurred in Wood), employer-employee immunity under workers compensation, and governmental immunity.

A particularly difficult question arises with respect to liability assigned to employers. Traditionally, the workers compensation statutes barred all claims against the employer. No matter how egregious the employer’s behavior in causing an employee’s injury, it was not involved in any way in a suit the employee might bring against other potentially responsible parties such as the manufacturer of an allegedly defective product that was also the cause of the plaintiff’s injury. With the advent of comparative fault, a number of jurisdictions allow other defendants to join the employer at least for the
808
purposes of permitting the fact finder to assign it a percentage of responsibility. At the same time, most jurisdictions continue to hold that the workers compensation statute either completely bars or substantially limits any contribution rights the other defendants may have against the employer, effectively making the employer’s percentage of responsibility a defaulted share. See Sullivan v. Scoular Grain Co. of Utah, 853 P.2d 877 (Utah 1993).

b.The unknown defendant. A problem can arise when one or more of the defendants are unknown. For example, in the hypothetical used above in which defendant A negligently ran a red light causing a collision with B who is then hit from behind by defendant C who was following too closely, assume that A left the scene without stopping and it has been impossible to identify A subsequently. Obviously, B cannot sue A. Does that mean that C will be stuck paying all of the damages? Traditionally that was the case, but with the advent of comparative responsibility, a few states have begun to treat A as a party for purposes of assigning a share of responsibility, and then treating whatever share the fact finder assigns to A as a defaulted share. The court would then redistribute that share by whatever method of reassignment prevails in that state. See Brown v. Keill, 580 P.2d 867 (Kan. 1978); Ariz. Rev. Stat. Ann.§ 12–2506(B) (2003); Colo. Rev. Stat. § 13–21–111.5(3) (2003).

c.The effect of a settlement and release. At common law a settlement with any defendant, which released that defendant from further liability, was viewed as satisfying the liability of all the joint defendants to the plaintiff and, therefore, such a settlement agreement released all defendants from further liability. As a result, the plaintiff could not settle with one defendant for part of the damages and then sue the other defendants for the remainder. The rule had the obvious effect of discouraging settlements unless all of the defendants were parties to the agreement. While following this common law rule, many courts tried to circumvent its effect in order to encourage settlements. One method developed for avoiding the harsh doctrine was the “covenant not to sue.” In return for a settlement from one joint tortfeasor, the plaintiff, rather than giving a release from liability, instead contracted not to bring an action on the underlying claim against that defendant. The need to resort to such technical devices has now been obviated by adoption of comparative fault and contribution statutes.

Most jurisdictions now follow some version of Section 4 of the Uniform Contribution Among Tortfeasors Act. A settling party has no contribution rights against non-settlers and, as long as the settlement was given in good faith, the non-settlers have no right of contribution against the settling party. However, some jurisdictions have abolished the good faith requirement. For example, the Nebraska statute discharging settlers from any contribution obligations does not contain a good faith limitation. Rev. Stat. Neb. § 25–21,185.11. Under what circumstances does a “good faith” requirement matter under comparative responsibility regimes? How is this tied to the issue of joint and several liability?

Because juries in comparative responsibility states are routinely asked to assign a percentage of responsibility to settling parties, courts must determine
809
what role this information should play in assessing the impact of a settlement on the liability of the non-settling parties. Most jurisdictions have settled on one of two approaches: a percentage reduction or a dollar-for-dollar reduction.

The percentage reduction approach ignores the actual amount of the settlement. Instead, it reduces the total amount all non-settling defendants owe the plaintiff by the percentage of responsibility assigned the settler by the fact finder. Assume, for example that a jury reached a verdict in which they found the plaintiff was not at fault and awarding her $300 in damages. They allocated responsibility among one settling party and two defendants at one-third each. Under this alternative, the remaining defendants would owe the plaintiff $200 dollars regardless of the dollar amount of the settlement.

One result of the percentage contribution reduction scheme is that the plaintiff may end up with more or less than a full recovery. If the sum of the settlement and the monies owed by the remaining defendants is more than the amount awarded by the fact finder, should the one satisfaction rule bar the plaintiff from receiving this total sum? In Charles v. Giant Eagle Markets, 522 A.2d 1 (Pa. 1987), the plaintiff reached settlement with one joint defendant. The jury then brought in a verdict against the other joint defendant. The settlement turned out to be for more than the settler would have owed based on the percentage of liability the jury assigned to the settler. The court held that the plaintiff was entitled to the share assigned to the non-settling defendant as well as the settlement amount negotiated with the settling defendant even though this produced a total recovery almost $4000 above the total jury award. This may be explained, in part, on the ground that the plaintiff takes the risk of a “too low” settlement and therefore should have the benefit when able to negotiate what turns out to be a very favorable settlement. In addition, why should the remaining defendant get the benefit of the plaintiff’s advantageous settlement?

The dollar-for-dollar approach (sometimes called a pro tanto approach) does the opposite. It ignores the percentage of responsibility assigned to the settler. Rather, it reduces the amount owed by the remaining defendants by the actual dollar amount of the settlement. To return to our example, if the settler actually settled with the plaintiff for $50 then the remaining jointly and severally liable defendants would owe the plaintiff $250. If, on the other hand, the settler settled for $150, the remaining defendants would owe the plaintiff only $150.

Note that if the settler settles for “too little,” that is for less than the settler owed according to the jury verdict, the choice between these alternatives is irrelevant to the severally liable defendant. She is responsible only for her percentage share. In this situation, the choice does matter to jointly and severally liable defendants. With respect to these individuals, the Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability § 16 adopts the percentage reduction approach. What are the pros and cons of this choice?

d.Procedures. Contribution statutes typically permit the action to be instituted in either the original action that produced the judgment, or in a
810
separate action subsequent to the original judgment. These statutes also have a statute of limitations for the contribution action, usually one year from the entry of a final judgment. Uniform Act § 3 details some of these enforcement matters.

6.Set-off. When a plaintiff is partially at fault, judgments may be entered against both plaintiff and defendants. It will be recalled that in Hoffman v. Jones, the court stated, supra, page 727, that, in a case where both the plaintiff and a counterclaiming defendant were injured and were both at fault, the trial judge should enter one judgment for the net amount due the party receiving the greatest verdict after subtracting (setting off) the amount of fault attributable to the other party. Where both parties are uninsured this certainly seems a reasonable way of handling the matter. Where they are insured against third party liability, however, the effect of this solution may be to give a windfall to the insurance companies. In Stuyvesant Insurance Co. v. Bournazian, 342 So.2d 471 (Fla.1976), the court declared that set-offs are only to be made when the parties are uninsured or to insured parties to the extent the amounts awarded exceed the policy limits. In a subsequent case, State Farm Mutual Automobile Insurance Co. v. Eberhardt, 374 So.2d 1113 (Fla.App.1979), an uninsured motorist brought an action against an insured motorist who counterclaimed. The uninsured motorist was awarded the larger verdict. The trial court accordingly set-off the verdicts and entered a judgment for the excess in favor of the uninsured motorist. The court of appeal reversed and directed that there should be no set-offs. Otherwise, in such a situation, an insured motorist might never recover anything. The Oregon (Or. Rev. Stat. 18.470) and Rhode Island (R.I. Gen. L. 1956 § 9–20–4.1) comparative fault statutes prohibit set-offs. The problem is dealt with in § 9 of the Restatement (Third) of Torts: Apportionment of Liability, which provides different rules depending upon the insurance coverage present.

